 A module is regarded as a complex concentrated in degree zero, so
 its shift by $s$ has cohomology in degree $-s$.
+For a $k$-algebra $A$, the category $\D(A\text{-}\Mod)$ is the
+localization of the category of integer-indexed cochain complexes of
+all left $A$-modules at quasi-isomorphisms, namely chain maps inducing
+isomorphisms on every cohomology module.  Write $q_A$ for this
+localization functor.  A complex is acyclic if all its cohomology
+modules vanish.  For complexes $U,V$ of left $A$-modules, a homotopy
+between chain maps $f,g:U\to V$ is a family of $A$-linear maps $h^n:U^n\to V^{n-1}$ satisfying
+$f-g=d_Vh+hd_U$; a map is null-homotopic if it is homotopic to zero.
+
+Let $A$ and $B$ be $k$-algebras.  A complex $M$ of
+$(B,A)$-bimodules has commuting left $B$- and right $A$-actions,
+agreeing on $k$, and bimodule-linear differentials.  For a complex
+$N$ of left $A$-modules, $M\otimes_A N$ denotes the direct-sum
+total complex of left $B$-modules.  For integers $p,q,n$, its terms
+and differential on $x\in M^p$, $y\in N^q$ are
+\[
+ (M\otimes_A N)^n=\bigoplus_{p+q=n}M^p\otimes_A N^q,
+ \qquad
+ d(x\otimes y)=d_Mx\otimes y+(-1)^p x\otimes d_Ny.
+\]
+Here the tensor products in the summands are ordinary balanced
+module tensor products over $A$.  For $a\in A$ the balancing
+relation is $(xa)\otimes y=x\otimes(ay)$.  For $b\in B$ the
+exterior action is $b(x\otimes y)=(bx)\otimes y$.
+
+A complex $Q$ of left $A$-modules is \emph{$K$-projective} if every
+chain map from $Q$ to an acyclic complex is null-homotopic.  Each
+complex $N$ admits a quasi-isomorphism
+$\varepsilon_N:Q_N\to N$ with $Q_N$ $K$-projective.  Such
+resolutions can be chosen functorially.  The symbol $\otL$ denotes
+the total left-derived tensor product.  For fixed $M$, localization
+gives a functor $L_M$ and natural comparison isomorphisms $c_N$
+with the following types:
+\[
+ \begin{gathered}
+ L_M=M\otL_A-:\D(A\text{-}\Mod)\longrightarrow
+                  \D(B\text{-}\Mod),\\
+ L_M(q_A N)\xrightarrow[\sim]{\,c_N\,}q_B(M\otimes_A Q_N).
+ \end{gathered}
+\]
+The maps
+$\eta_N=q_B(M\otimes_A\varepsilon_N)\circ c_N$ define a natural
+transformation
+\[
+ \eta:L_Mq_A\longrightarrow q_B(M\otimes_A-).
+\]
+Its defining universal property is the following.  For any functor
+$G:\D(A\text{-}\Mod)\to\D(B\text{-}\Mod)$ and natural
+transformation $\theta:Gq_A\to q_B(M\otimes_A-)$, there is a
+unique natural transformation $\sigma:G\to L_M$ such that
+$\eta_N\circ\sigma_{q_A N}=\theta_N$ for every $N$.  Thus the
+left-derived functor is a right Kan extension along $q_A$.
+
+\begin{proof}[Existence and well-definedness]
+One may construct $Q_N$ as follows.  Let $Q_0$ be the free graded
+left $A$-module with one generator of degree $n$ for every integer
+$n$ and cycle $z\in N^n$.  Give these generators zero differential
+and map the generator indexed by $z$ to $z$.  Inductively, suppose
+that a chain map $\varepsilon_i:Q_i\to N$ has been defined for
+an integer $i\geq0$.  For every integer $n$ and pair
+$z\in Q_i^n$, $y\in N^{n-1}$ satisfying
+$d_{Q_i}z=0$ and $\varepsilon_i(z)=d_Ny$, adjoin a free generator
+$e_{z,y}$ of degree $n-1$, set $de_{z,y}=z$, and extend the map
+by $\varepsilon_{i+1}(e_{z,y})=y$.  The resulting inclusions
+$Q_i\subset Q_{i+1}$ are split as graded modules.  Set
+$Q_N=\bigcup_{i\geq0}Q_i$, with the induced differential and
+augmentation $\varepsilon_N$.  Every cohomology class of $N$
+is represented by an initial generator.  A cycle in $Q_N$ lies in
+some finite stage; if its image is a boundary in $N$, the next
+stage makes it a boundary.  Hence $\varepsilon_N$ is a
+quasi-isomorphism.  Indexing generators by all the displayed cycles
+and pairs makes this construction functorial in chain maps $N\to N'$.
+
+To verify $K$-projectivity, let $u:Q_N\to C$ be a chain map with
+$C$ acyclic.  Construct a degree $-1$ homotopy $h$ stage by stage.
+For a new generator $e$ of degree $n$, its differential lies in
+the preceding stage, where $h$ is already defined.  The element
+$u(e)-h(de)$ is a cycle of degree $n$ in $C$.  Choose $h(e)$
+of degree $n-1$ with $d_Ch(e)=u(e)-h(de)$ and extend
+$A$-linearly.  Starting with the zero preceding stage for $Q_0$
+gives $u=d_Ch+hd_{Q_N}$ on the union.
+
+Maps from a $K$-projective complex lift along quasi-isomorphisms
+uniquely up to homotopy, as follows by applying maps out of that
+complex to the acyclic mapping cone.  For a chain map $f:U\to V$,
+this cone has degree-$n$ term $V^n\oplus U^{n+1}$ and differential
+$(v,u)\mapsto(d_Vv+f(u),-d_Uu)$.  Consequently,
+quasi-isomorphisms between $K$-projective complexes are homotopy
+equivalences, and any two such resolutions of $N$ are homotopy
+equivalent over $N$ up to homotopy.  An input homotopy $h$ induces the tensor homotopy
+$x\otimes y\mapsto(-1)^p x\otimes h(y)$ for $x\in M^p$;
+thus tensoring with $M$ preserves chain homotopies.  The functor $N\mapsto M\otimes_A Q_N$
+therefore sends quasi-isomorphisms to quasi-isomorphisms and
+descends to $L_M$; the comparison and $\eta$ above are induced by
+localization and the augmentations.  Given $G$ and $\theta$, the
+factorization on $q_A N$ is the composite
+\[
+ \begin{aligned}
+ G(q_A N)&\xrightarrow{\,G(q_A\varepsilon_N)^{-1}\,}
+ G(q_A Q_N)\xrightarrow{\,\theta_{Q_N}\,}q_B(M\otimes_A Q_N)\\
+ &\xrightarrow{\,c_N^{-1}\,}L_M(q_A N).
+ \end{aligned}
+\]
+Naturality gives the required factorization identity.  The transformation $\eta$
+is an isomorphism on $K$-projective inputs, so naturality with
+respect to $\varepsilon_N$ makes this factorization unique.
+\end{proof}
+
+A complex of right $A$-modules is \emph{$K$-flat} if its total
+tensor product with every acyclic complex of left $A$-modules is
+acyclic.  A bounded complex of projective right $A$-modules is
+$K$-flat: its finite filtration by degree has shifted projective
+modules as successive quotients, and tensoring these quotients
+with an acyclic complex remains acyclic.  Thus, when $M$ is such
+a bounded complex, $M\otimes_A N$ itself computes $M\otL_A N$
+for every $N$.  Bounded-above complexes of projective left
+$A$-modules are $K$-projective by descending lifting of homotopies,
+so any quasi-isomorphism from such a complex to $N$ may be used
+as $\varepsilon_N$.  If $M$ and $N$ are bounded and termwise
+finite-dimensional over $k$, with $M$ termwise right projective,
+the result belongs to $\D^b(B\text{-}\modu)$.
+
+Call a complex semi-free if it has homogeneous free generators
+adjoined in stages, with each generator's differential in earlier
+stages.  If $C$ is a further $k$-algebra and $N$ is a complex of
+$(A,C)$-bimodules, its resolution may be constructed over
+$A\otimes_k C^{\mathrm{op}}$, where $C^{\mathrm{op}}$ is the
+algebra with reversed multiplication.  The left action is defined as follows.  For $c\in C$, let $c^{\mathrm{op}}$
+denote its image in $C^{\mathrm{op}}$.  For $a\in A$, $c\in C$,
+$j\in\Z$ and $v\in N^j$, set $(a\otimes c^{\mathrm{op}})v=avc$.
+The underlying left $A$-complex of this resolution is still semi-free: choose a $k$-basis of $C$ to
+express each freely adjoined $A\otimes_k C^{\mathrm{op}}$-module
+as a direct sum of free left $A$-modules.
+After forgetting the right $C$-action this computes the previously
+defined derived tensor.  For $b\in B$ and $c\in C$ the retained
+exterior actions are $b(x\otimes y)c=(bx)\otimes(yc)$.
+It then gives an object of
+$\D((B\otimes_k C^{\mathrm{op}})\text{-}\Mod)$.\footnote{For the derived tensor functor
+over noncommutative algebras, viewed as differential graded
+algebras concentrated in degree zero, see the Stacks Project,
+Lemma 22.33.2, \url{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09LS};
+compare the resolutions in Lemma 22.20.4,
+\url{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09KP}.  Passing to
+opposite algebras converts its right-module convention to the
+left-module convention used here.}
+
 Path products and endomorphism products are composed from right to left.
 
